      "content": "<h3>What is a Stack?</h3>\n<p>A <strong>stack</strong> is a pile where you only touch the <strong>top</strong>. The last item you put on is the first item you take off: <strong>LIFO</strong> (Last In, First Out). Think of a pile of plates, or the Back button of a browser: the page you visited most recently is the first one you go back to.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The class <code>java.util.Stack</code> extends <code>Vector</code>, so it is a growable array in which the <strong>end</strong> of the array is the <strong>top</strong> of the stack.</li>\n<li>Only five methods are special: <code>push</code>, <code>pop</code>, <code>peek</code>, <code>empty</code> and <code>search</code>.</li>\n<li>Because it extends Vector, it is <strong>synchronized</strong> (thread-safe, slower) and also exposes all Vector/List methods (<code>get(i)</code>, <code>add(i, e)</code>, ...), which can break the LIFO discipline.</li>\n<li>Allows duplicates and <code>null</code>. Starts with capacity 10 and doubles when full.</li>\n<li><strong>Modern advice:</strong> the Java documentation itself recommends <code>Deque&lt;E&gt; stack = new ArrayDeque&lt;&gt;()</code> for new code. Learn <code>Stack</code> for interviews and to read old code.</li>\n</ul>"
